{"summary": "I finished `transpose` in `transpose.py`. The project has no test files, so I checked it by hand on a few inputs from the instructions, and each gave the expected output: `"ABC\nDE"` gives `"AD\nBE\nC"`, `"AB\nDEF"` gives `"AD\nBE\n F"`, an empty input gives `""`, and the triangle example comes out right.

It works by padding each row on the right with spaces until it is as long as the longest row at or below it. Then it builds each output row from one column, taking only the input rows that reach that far. Gaps get filled with spaces on the left, nothing is added on the right, and spaces already in the input are kept. It uses only the standard library.

One thing to know: if the input ends with a newline, that is read as one last empty line. So `"A1\n"` gives `"A\n1"`."}
